  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:5725",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the interaction with leukemia cells through a series of molecular pathways, primarily through its targeting of the Geminin protein and other members of the Solute carrier organic anion transporter family. These targets are ultimately directed toward leukemia cells, suggesting a potential role in modulating cellular processes critical to leukemia progression. The evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, with benzocaine acting as a target for a complex, highly conserved protein that is a key regulator of cell cycle and apoptosis. The presence of a common downstream target, [(3S,9S,12R,13R,14R,15R)-13-hydroxy-6-(1-hydroxyethyl)-12,14-dimethyl-2,5,8,11-tetraoxo-9-(propan-2-yl)-15-tridecyl-1-oxa-4,7,10-triazacyclopentadecan-3-yl]methyl acetate, indicates a shared mechanism of action. This suggests that benzocaine may exert its effects by targeting a central regulatory protein, which in turn influences leukemia cell behavior. The integration of these findings highlights a potential mechanism by which benzocaine could interact with leukemia cells, emphasizing the importance of the Geminin and related proteins in the context of cancer biology.",
